.TH CBMAP_DELETE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
cbmap_delete \- Delete a key-value pair from a CBMap

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/cbmap_internal.h>

.IB int
.IB cbmap_delete (cbmap_t map, void* key, size_t key_len);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcbmap_delete\fR function is used to delete a key-value pair from a CBMap. This function removes the key and its associated value from the map and frees the memory allocated for the key and value.

The function takes three parameters:
.TP
.B map
A pointer to the CBMap from which the key-value pair should be deleted.
.TP
.B key
A pointer to the key to be deleted.
.TP
.B key_len
The length of the key.

The function returns one of the following values:
.TP
.B NOT_FOUND
The key was not found in the map.
.TP
.B FOUND
The key was successfully deleted from the map.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcbmap_delete\fR function to delete a key-value pair from a CBMap:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/cbmap_internal.h>

int main() {
    cbmap_t map = cbmap_new();
    void* key = ...;  // Initialize key
    size_t key_len = ...;  // Initialize key length

    // Assume map is initialized and used somewhere

    // Delete the key-value pair
    int result = cbmap_delete(map, key, key_len);

    if(result == NOT_FOUND) {
        printf("Key not found\n");
    } else if(result == FOUND) {
        printf("Key deleted successfully\n");
    }

    cbmap_free(map);

    return 0;
}
.nef